---
// src/pages/my-path/index.astro
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---
<Base
  title="My Path — Skovgard 2026"
  description="How Jimmy Skovgard and Kevin built this campaign website from scratch — every step, every tool, every command. A replicable guide for the next candidate."
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="pt-24 pb-20 px-4">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">

      <!-- Header -->
      <p class="text-wy-gold font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sm mb-2">Open Playbook</p>
      <h1 class="text-5xl font-serif font-bold text-wy-navy mb-6">My Path</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        This is how we built this website: a U.S. Senate candidate and a neighbor, no agency, no budget,
        no prior web experience. Every step is documented so the next Wyoming candidate can replicate it
        in a weekend.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mb-10">
        Want help getting started? See <a href="/support/" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">Campaign-to-Campaign Support</a> or
        email <a href="mailto:jimmy@grassrootsmvt.org?subject=Website%20Help%20Please" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">jimmy@grassrootsmvt.org</a>.
      </p>

      <!-- Table of Contents -->
      <nav class="bg-wy-stone border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-6 mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-bold text-wy-navy mb-3">Steps</h2>
        <ol class="space-y-1 text-sm">
          <li><a href="#step-1" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">1. Choose and Register a Domain Name</a></li>
          <li><a href="#step-2" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">2. Create a Free Cloudflare Account for Hosting and DNS</a></li>
          <li><a href="#step-3" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">3. Point the Domain's Nameservers to Cloudflare</a></li>
          <li><a href="#step-4" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">4. Create a GitHub Account and a New Repository</a></li>
          <li><a href="#step-5" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">5. Choose a Development Setup (WSL + Ubuntu + VS Code)</a></li>
          <li><a href="#step-6" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">6. Scaffold the Website From the Skovgard2026 Template</a></li>
          <li><a href="#step-7" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">7. Publish the Site With Cloudflare Pages</a></li>
          <li><a href="#step-8" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">8. Connect the Custom Domain to Cloudflare Pages</a></li>
          <li><a href="#step-9" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">9. Document Everything So the Next Candidate Can Repeat It</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <!-- STEP 1 -->
      <section id="step-1" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-wy-navy text-wy-gold font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-lg mt-1">1</span>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-wy-navy">Choose and Register a Domain Name</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-600 mb-6">
          <p>
            Your domain is your address on the internet. <code>skovgard2026.com</code> is an example. Pick something
            simple, predictable, and easy to say out loud. Avoid hyphens. Include the year if your name is common.
          </p>
          <p>
            We used <strong>Namecheap</strong>. Porkbun is another solid option. Both offer free WHOIS privacy
            (so your home address doesn't appear in public records) and cost around $10–15 per year for a <code>.com</code>.
          </p>
          <h3>What to do</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Go to <a href="https://www.namecheap.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">namecheap.com</a> and search for your domain.</li>
            <li>Add it to your cart and check out. Enable WHOIS privacy (usually free).</li>
            <li>Save your login credentials somewhere safe. You will need them in Step 3.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-gold rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
          <strong>Prompt A</strong>: Ask an AI assistant: <em>"I'm a first-time candidate registering a domain name on Namecheap. Walk me through the checkout process step by step, including enabling WHOIS privacy."</em>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- STEP 2 -->
      <section id="step-2" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-wy-navy text-wy-gold font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-lg mt-1">2</span>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-wy-navy">Create a Free Cloudflare Account for Hosting and DNS</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-600 mb-6">
          <p>
            Cloudflare does two things for us: it manages our DNS (the system that connects your domain to your
            website) and it hosts the site for free through Cloudflare Pages.
          </p>
          <p>
            The free tier is generous: unlimited bandwidth, global CDN, automatic HTTPS, and 500 builds per month.
            You will never pay a hosting bill.
          </p>
          <h3>What to do</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Go to <a href="https://dash.cloudflare.com/sign-up" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">dash.cloudflare.com/sign-up</a> and create a free account.</li>
            <li>Verify your email address.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Add a Site</strong> and enter your domain name.</li>
            <li>Select the <strong>Free plan</strong>.</li>
            <li>Cloudflare will scan your existing DNS records. Note the two nameservers it gives you. You will need them in Step 3.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-gold rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
          <strong>Prompt A</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"I just created a Cloudflare account and added my domain. It gave me two nameservers. What do I do with them, and how long will it take for DNS to propagate?"</em>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- STEP 3 -->
      <section id="step-3" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-wy-navy text-wy-gold font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-lg mt-1">3</span>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-wy-navy">Point the Domain's Nameservers to Cloudflare</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-600 mb-6">
          <p>
            Your domain registrar (Namecheap) controls which DNS provider handles your domain. Right now it uses
            Namecheap's own nameservers. You need to switch them to Cloudflare's.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is a one-time change and takes 5–30 minutes to propagate. Once done, Cloudflare is in charge of
            your DNS and can point your domain at your Cloudflare Pages site.
          </p>
          <h3>What to do on Namecheap</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Log in to Namecheap and go to your domain list.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Manage</strong> next to your domain.</li>
            <li>Under <strong>Nameservers</strong>, choose <strong>Custom DNS</strong>.</li>
            <li>Enter the two nameservers Cloudflare gave you (e.g., <code>lara.ns.cloudflare.com</code>).</li>
            <li>Save. Wait 15–30 minutes, then check Cloudflare's dashboard. It will say "Active" when propagation is complete.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-gold rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
          <strong>Prompt A</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"On Namecheap, how do I update my domain's nameservers to use Cloudflare's nameservers? Show me the exact steps in the Namecheap dashboard."</em>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- STEP 4 -->
      <section id="step-4" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-wy-navy text-wy-gold font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-lg mt-1">4</span>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-wy-navy">Create a GitHub Account and a New Repository</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-600 mb-6">
          <p>
            GitHub stores your website's code. Every time you push a change, Cloudflare Pages detects it and
            automatically rebuilds and republishes your site. This is the core workflow.
          </p>
          <p>
            A repository (repo) is just a folder that tracks changes over time. Think of it as a shared folder
            with a detailed history of every edit ever made.
          </p>
          <h3>What to do</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Go to <a href="https://github.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">github.com</a> and create a free account.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>New repository</strong>. Name it something like <code>yourname2026</code>.</li>
            <li>Set visibility to <strong>Public</strong> (required for the free Cloudflare Pages integration) or Private (paid plan).</li>
            <li>Do NOT initialize with a README. You will push your own code in Step 6.</li>
            <li>Note your repo URL: <code>https://github.com/yourusername/yourname2026</code>.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-gold rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <strong>Prompt A</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"I'm new to GitHub. Walk me through creating a new public repository for my campaign website, including setting up Git on my computer for the first time."</em>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-sky rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <strong>Prompt B</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"What's the difference between git commit and git push? Explain it like I'm not a developer."</em>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- STEP 5 -->
      <section id="step-5" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-wy-navy text-wy-gold font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-lg mt-1">5</span>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-wy-navy">Choose a Development Setup (WSL + Ubuntu + VS Code)</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-600 mb-6">
          <p>
            We used a Windows laptop with WSL (Windows Subsystem for Linux). WSL lets you run a full Linux
            environment inside Windows with no separate machine needed. Ubuntu is the Linux distribution we chose,
            and VS Code is the code editor.
          </p>
          <p>
            This combination is free, well-documented, and the same setup used by professional developers worldwide.
            If you're on a Mac, you can skip WSL and use your Terminal app directly.
          </p>
          <h3>Windows setup</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Open PowerShell as Administrator and run: <code>wsl --install</code>. Restart when prompted.</li>
            <li>Open the Microsoft Store, search <strong>Ubuntu 22.04</strong>, and install it.</li>
            <li>Launch Ubuntu, create a username and password when prompted.</li>
            <li>Install Node.js via nvm: follow the instructions at <a href="https://github.com/nvm-sh/nvm" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">github.com/nvm-sh/nvm</a>.</li>
            <li>Install VS Code from <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-wy-gold hover:underline">code.visualstudio.com</a>. Add the <strong>WSL</strong> extension.</li>
            <li>From Ubuntu, run <code>code .</code> in your project folder to open VS Code connected to WSL.</li>
          </ol>
          <h3>Mac setup</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Open Terminal. Install Homebrew: <code>/bin/bash -c "$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Homebrew/install/HEAD/install.sh)"</code></li>
            <li>Install nvm: <code>brew install nvm</code> and follow the shell config steps.</li>
            <li>Install Node 22: <code>nvm install 22 && nvm use 22</code></li>
            <li>Install VS Code from the website.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-gold rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <strong>Prompt A</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"I installed WSL and Ubuntu on Windows. Walk me through installing nvm and Node.js 22 inside Ubuntu, and verifying the installation works."</em>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-sky rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <strong>Prompt B</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"I'm using VS Code with the WSL extension. How do I open a project folder from my Ubuntu filesystem in VS Code?"</em>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- STEP 6 -->
      <section id="step-6" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-wy-navy text-wy-gold font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-lg mt-1">6</span>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-wy-navy">Scaffold the Website From the Skovgard2026 Template</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-600 mb-6">
          <p>
            This site uses <strong>Astro</strong>, a modern static site generator, with <strong>Tailwind CSS</strong>
            for styling. Astro compiles your pages into plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, which means the site loads
            fast and has no server to maintain.
          </p>
          <p>
            You can fork our GitHub repository and replace the content with your own. Or start a fresh Astro project
            and copy our component patterns.
          </p>
          <h3>Fork approach (recommended)</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Go to our GitHub repo (link on request: email jimmy@grassrootsmvt.org).</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Fork</strong> to copy it to your account.</li>
            <li>Clone it to your local machine: <code>git clone https://github.com/yourusername/yourfork</code></li>
            <li>In your project folder: <code>npm install</code> then <code>npm run dev</code></li>
            <li>Open <code>http://localhost:4321</code> in your browser. You should see the site.</li>
            <li>Edit <code>src/pages/index.astro</code> to start customizing content.</li>
          </ol>
          <h3>Key files to change</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><code>src/layouts/Base.astro</code>: site-wide title, meta tags, fonts</li>
            <li><code>src/components/Nav.astro</code>: navigation links and logo</li>
            <li><code>src/components/Footer.astro</code>: footer content and links</li>
            <li><code>tailwind.config.mjs</code>: brand colors</li>
            <li><code>public/</code>: images, PDFs, and other static files</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-gold rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <strong>Prompt A</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"I forked an Astro repository and cloned it locally. I ran npm install and npm run dev. The dev server is running. How do I change the site title, colors, and navigation links?"</em>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-sky rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <strong>Prompt B</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"In Tailwind CSS v4, how do I define custom brand colors so I can use them as class names like bg-my-navy or text-my-gold?"</em>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-amber-300 rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <strong>Prompt C</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"I'm building a campaign website in Astro. Help me create a reusable Nav component with a logo on the left and navigation links on the right, with a mobile hamburger menu."</em>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-gray-400 rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <strong>Prompt D</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"My Astro site is working locally. How do I commit my changes to git and push them to GitHub?"</em>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- STEP 7 -->
      <section id="step-7" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-wy-navy text-wy-gold font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-lg mt-1">7</span>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-wy-navy">Publish the Site With Cloudflare Pages</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-600 mb-6">
          <p>
            Cloudflare Pages connects directly to your GitHub repository. Every time you push a commit, Cloudflare
            automatically runs your build command and publishes the result. There's no FTP, no server config, no
            manual upload.
          </p>
          <h3>What to do</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>In the Cloudflare dashboard, go to <strong>Pages</strong> and click <strong>Create a project</strong>.</li>
            <li>Choose <strong>Connect to Git</strong> and authorize Cloudflare to access your GitHub account.</li>
            <li>Select your campaign repository.</li>
            <li>Set the build configuration:
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Framework preset:</strong> Astro</li>
                <li><strong>Build command:</strong> <code>npm run build</code></li>
                <li><strong>Build output directory:</strong> <code>dist</code></li>
              </ul>
            </li>
            <li>Click <strong>Save and Deploy</strong>. The first build takes about 2 minutes.</li>
            <li>Cloudflare gives you a URL like <code>yourrepo.pages.dev</code>. Visit it. Your site is live.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-gold rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <strong>Prompt A</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"My Cloudflare Pages build failed. Here is the build log: [paste log here]. What went wrong and how do I fix it?"</em>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-sky rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <strong>Prompt B</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"How do I set environment variables in Cloudflare Pages for my Astro site?"</em>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- STEP 8 -->
      <section id="step-8" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-wy-navy text-wy-gold font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-lg mt-1">8</span>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-wy-navy">Connect the Custom Domain to Cloudflare Pages</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-600 mb-6">
          <p>
            Right now your site is live at a <code>.pages.dev</code> URL. This step connects your real domain
            (<code>yourname2026.com</code>) so voters can find you at the address on your flyers.
          </p>
          <p>
            Because your domain is already managed by Cloudflare (from Step 3), this is almost instant. No
            waiting for DNS propagation.
          </p>
          <h3>What to do</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>In your Cloudflare Pages project, go to <strong>Custom Domains</strong>.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Set up a custom domain</strong>.</li>
            <li>Enter your domain: <code>yourname2026.com</code></li>
            <li>Cloudflare automatically adds the necessary DNS records since it manages your domain.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Activate domain</strong>.</li>
            <li>Wait 1–5 minutes. Visit your domain. Your site should appear with a valid HTTPS certificate.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Also add <code>www.yourname2026.com</code> as a second custom domain and set up a redirect from
            <code>www</code> to the root domain (or vice versa).
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-gold rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
          <strong>Prompt A</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"I've added my custom domain to Cloudflare Pages but it's showing a certificate error. The domain is managed by Cloudflare. What could cause this and how do I fix it?"</em>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- STEP 9 -->
      <section id="step-9" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-wy-navy text-wy-gold font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-lg mt-1">9</span>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-wy-navy">Document Everything So the Next Candidate Can Repeat It</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-600 mb-6">
          <p>
            This is the step most people skip. Don't skip it.
          </p>
          <p>
            While everything is fresh, right after you finish a step, write down exactly what you did. Not a
            summary. The actual steps, the actual commands, the actual settings. What confused you. What you had
            to Google. What worked and what didn't.
          </p>
          <p>
            You are not just building a campaign website. You are building a movement's infrastructure. The next
            candidate in your county, the one who can't afford to hire a web agency, the one who's never done
            this before, is going to follow your notes.
          </p>
          <h3>What to document</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Every account you created and what email you used</li>
            <li>Every setting that wasn't obvious</li>
            <li>The exact build command and Node version that worked</li>
            <li>Any errors you hit and how you fixed them</li>
            <li>How to update content after launch</li>
            <li>Login credentials (stored securely, using a password manager like Bitwarden)</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Where to keep it</h3>
          <p>
            A simple Google Doc or Notion page works fine. Share it with the campaign's email account so it
            doesn't live only on your personal computer. When you hand off the campaign, hand off the document.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-gold rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <strong>Prompt A</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"Help me write a handoff document for a campaign website built on Astro and Cloudflare Pages. The next person has no technical background. What should I include?"</em>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-wy-stone border-l-4 border-wy-sky rounded-r-xl p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <strong>Prompt B</strong>: Ask an AI: <em>"What's the minimum I need to document so that someone else could take over managing this website without my help?"</em>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Call to Action -->
      <section class="bg-wy-navy text-white rounded-2xl p-10 mt-4">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-serif font-bold mb-4 text-wy-gold">The Citizen's Oath</h2>
        <div class="prose prose-lg prose-invert text-gray-300 mb-6">
          <p>
            We built this to prove it's possible. A Senate campaign, two guys, a laptop, and a weekend.
            No agency. No budget. No gatekeepers.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you run for office in Wyoming, or anywhere, you deserve a platform that looks professional
            and costs you nothing. Democracy should not have a paywall.
          </p>
          <p>
            So here's the deal: if we help you build your site, you help the next person build theirs.
            Document your steps. Share what you learned. Pass the playbook forward.
          </p>
          <p class="text-white font-semibold text-lg mt-4">
            That's how citizens take their government back.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
          <a
            href="/support/"
            class="inline-block bg-wy-gold text-wy-navy font-bold px-8 py-4 rounded text-lg hover:bg-amber-400 transition-colors text-center"
          >
            Get Help From Jimmy →
          </a>
          <a
            href="mailto:jimmy@grassrootsmvt.org?subject=Website%20Help%20Please"
            class="inline-block border-2 border-wy-gold text-wy-gold font-bold px-8 py-4 rounded text-lg hover:bg-wy-gold hover:text-wy-navy transition-colors text-center"
          >
            Email Directly →
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
